From: LuneOS <team@webos-ports.org>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 18:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] gstreamer: survive tearing a camera down with requests in
 flight

Destroying a libcamerasrc while requests are still in flight crashes or hangs
the application. Four separate problems contribute, all in the same area.

1. The requestCompleted signal is never disconnected. gst_libcamera_src_open()
   does

       cam->requestCompleted.connect(self->state, &GstLibcameraSrcState::requestCompleted);

   and GstLibcameraSrcState is a plain struct, not a libcamera::Object, so the
   connection is not dropped automatically when finalize() deletes the state.
   gst_libcamera_src_close() releases the camera without disconnecting, and the
   Camera outlives the element because the CameraManager holds it, so a
   completion arriving afterwards - a late metadata callback from an isolated
   IPA, say - runs on freed memory:

       #0  std::_Map_base<..., libcamera::ControlValue>::operator[]
       #1  libcamera::ControlList::find(unsigned int)
       #3  libcamera::ControlList::merge(...)
       #4  GstLibcameraSrcState::requestCompleted(libcamera::Request *)
       #7  libcamera::ipa::rkisp1::IPAProxyRkISP1Isolated::metadataReadyHandler(...)

2. requestCompleted() merges a request's metadata before checking whether the
   request was cancelled. PipelineHandler::stop() cancels and completes every
   in-flight request, so this runs for requests that never produced anything,
   on the camera manager thread, while the device is being torn down:

       #3  libcamera::ControlList::merge(...)
       #4  GstLibcameraSrcState::requestCompleted(libcamera::Request *)
       #6  libcamera::V4L2VideoDevice::streamOff()
       #7  libcamera::RkISP1Path::stop()
       #9  libcamera::PipelineHandler::stop(libcamera::Camera *)

3. Completions are assumed to arrive in the order the requests were queued:

       wrap = std::move(queuedRequests_.front());
       queuedRequests_.pop();
       g_return_if_fail(wrap->request_.get() == request);

   which does not hold around a stop. A completion can arrive once the queue
   has drained - front() on an empty std::queue being undefined behaviour - and
   otherwise consumes an unrelated wrapper, leaving two requests mishandled and

       assertion 'wrap->request_.get() == request' failed

   repeating until the pipeline stalls.

4. clearRequests() empties only completedRequests_, so a wrapper still queued
   when a session ends is carried into the next one.

Disconnect the signal before releasing the camera, skip the metadata of a
cancelled request, look the wrapper up by the request it belongs to, ignore a
completion that has none, and drop the queued wrappers when a session ends.
queuedRequests_ becomes a std::deque so a matching entry can be erased from the
middle.

All of this is reachable from an ordinary application: switching between the
front and back sensors of a PinePhone Pro destroys one libcamerasrc and creates
another on the same pipeline handler. Before this change the preview locked up
and the process died with SIGSEGV.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
diff -ruN gs72_a/src/gstreamer/gstlibcamerasrc.cpp gs72_b/src/gstreamer/gstlibcamerasrc.cpp
--- a/src/gstreamer/gstlibcamerasrc.cpp
+++ b/src/gstreamer/gstlibcamerasrc.cpp
@@ -21,7 +21,9 @@
 
 #include "gstlibcamerasrc.h"
 
+#include <algorithm>
 #include <atomic>
+#include <deque>
 #include <queue>
 #include <tuple>
 #include <utility>
@@ -122,7 +124,7 @@
 	 * gst_pad_query()).
 	 */
 	GMutex lock_;
-	std::queue<std::unique_ptr<RequestWrap>> queuedRequests_;
+	std::deque<std::unique_ptr<RequestWrap>> queuedRequests_;
 	std::queue<std::unique_ptr<RequestWrap>> completedRequests_;
 
 	ControlList initControls_;
@@ -215,7 +217,7 @@
 	{
 		GLibLocker locker(&lock_);
 		cam_->queueRequest(wrap->request_.get());
-		queuedRequests_.push(std::move(wrap));
+		queuedRequests_.push_back(std::move(wrap));
 	}
 
 	/* The RequestWrap will be deleted in the completion handler. */
@@ -229,18 +231,45 @@
 
 	std::unique_ptr<RequestWrap> wrap;
 
+	const bool cancelled = request->status() == Request::RequestCancelled;
+
 	{
 		GLibLocker locker(&lock_);
 
-		controls_.readMetadata(request);
+		/*
+		 * Only a request that actually ran carries usable metadata.
+		 * Every in-flight request is cancelled and completed from
+		 * PipelineHandler::stop(), and merging the metadata of one of
+		 * those while the camera is being torn down crashes in
+		 * ControlList::merge().
+		 */
+		if (!cancelled)
+			controls_.readMetadata(request);
 
-		wrap = std::move(queuedRequests_.front());
-		queuedRequests_.pop();
-	}
+		/*
+		 * Take the wrapper belonging to this request rather than
+		 * whatever happens to be at the head of the queue. Completions
+		 * are not guaranteed to arrive in the order the requests were
+		 * queued, and one can arrive after the queue has been drained,
+		 * for a request cancelled by stop() whose completion was
+		 * already on its way. front() on an empty queue is undefined
+		 * behaviour, and consuming an unrelated wrapper leaves both
+		 * requests mishandled.
+		 */
+		auto it = std::find_if(queuedRequests_.begin(), queuedRequests_.end(),
+				       [request](const std::unique_ptr<RequestWrap> &w) {
+					       return w->request_.get() == request;
+				       });
+		if (it == queuedRequests_.end()) {
+			GST_DEBUG_OBJECT(src_, "Completion for an unknown request, ignoring");
+			return;
+		}
 
-	g_return_if_fail(wrap->request_.get() == request);
+		wrap = std::move(*it);
+		queuedRequests_.erase(it);
+	}
 
-	if ((request->status() == Request::RequestCancelled)) {
+	if (cancelled) {
 		GST_DEBUG_OBJECT(src_, "Request was cancelled");
 		return;
 	}
@@ -453,6 +482,12 @@
 void GstLibcameraSrcState::clearRequests()
 {
 	GLibLocker locker(&lock_);
+	/*
+	 * Drop the queued requests as well. They belong to the session that has
+	 * just been stopped and cam_->stop() has cancelled them, so leaving
+	 * them behind carries dead wrappers into the next session.
+	 */
+	queuedRequests_.clear();
 	completedRequests_ = {};
 }
 
@@ -960,6 +995,17 @@
 
 	GST_DEBUG_OBJECT(self, "Releasing resources");
 
+	/*
+	 * Stop listening before giving the camera up. GstLibcameraSrcState is a
+	 * plain struct rather than a libcamera::Object, so the connection made
+	 * in gst_libcamera_src_open() is not torn down automatically when the
+	 * state is deleted in finalize(). The Camera outlives this element -
+	 * the CameraManager keeps it alive - so a completion delivered after
+	 * the element has gone, for instance a late metadata callback from an
+	 * isolated IPA, would call requestCompleted() on freed memory.
+	 */
+	state->cam_->requestCompleted.disconnect(state);
+
 	state->config_.reset();
 
 	ret = state->cam_->release();
